Yes. Reading it as a maze instead of a sequence changes what falls out of it.

Drop into the middle at:

“I want the quality of life of the emperor, without the burden of being emperor.”

Now spread outward.

One branch goes toward historical equivalence: not “who would Brandon literally have been in Rome?” but “what social function, training rarity, autonomy, status, access, danger, and material comfort would occupy a comparable percentile?” That lets you compare lives by relative position inside their civilizations, not by surface possessions.

Another branch goes toward Black Mirror / hindsight-forward: imagine a historian in 2226 describing your current life. Suddenly things we experience as ordinary—antibiotics, refrigeration, instant communication, aircraft, climate control, global information access, AI—become imperial-level powers scattered across ordinary households. The mirror reveals how much “emperor” we already possess without feeling imperial.

A third branch goes toward your actual desire. Strip away the Roman costume and you do not principally want a throne. You want some mixture of:

optionality + autonomy + material abundance + reach + security + time + access + ability to cause useful change

while subtracting:

administrative burden + constant exposure + succession politics + enemies + ceremonial obligation + responsibility for everyone else.

That is much more actionable than “become powerful,” because now it is a design specification.

Then another path crosses the earlier Golden String. Historically, many privileges of rulers have been technologically democratized:

messengers → telecommunications
court musicians → streaming
private libraries → internet
personal scribes → computers/AI
imperial maps → GPS
armies of clerks → software automation
physicians reserved for elites → mass medical systems

So perhaps the recurring historical trajectory is:

elite capability → infrastructure → abstraction → falling marginal cost → mass availability

That means your “emperor without emperorship” idea is not weird at all. Civilization has repeatedly done exactly that: extract the capability from the status position and distribute it.

Now enter from another random point:

“what I would look like in Rome.”

That branches into identity. Your modern labels—SEAL, veteran, father, mortgage professional, whatever else—would not translate directly. But the underlying functions might: selected warrior, officer, navigator, strategist, trusted specialist, household head, intermediary, adviser.

So the translation operator is:

modern identity → strip institutions → recover functions → instantiate under historical constraints

That can be run across eras. And if something survives Rome, medieval Japan, 1800s America, and a speculative 2200, it is probably closer to a deep identity invariant than a job title.

Then your token point: yes. Once the words become a relational representation, sequential order becomes optional. We can traverse by association instead of sentence position:

emperor ↔ burden ↔ power ↔ technology ↔ democratization ↔ quality of life ↔ historical percentile ↔ identity ↔ future hindsight ↔ distributed capability

That is the Golden Thread.

And there’s a particularly useful output from the whole maze:

History can be treated as a library of capability bundles.

Instead of asking “Would I rather live in Rome?”, ask:

Which capabilities did emperors have? Which did ordinary people lack? Which have now become cheap? Which elite capabilities remain scarce today? What mechanism would democratize those next?

That last question turns the imaginary mirror into a technology and opportunity detector.

So yes—“it’s all buildable” is directionally right, provided we mean the underlying functions. We do not need to recreate emperors.

We can keep extracting the things that once required an emperor and make them available without requiring anyone to become one.